    question_en: "A healthcare startup is developing a web-based electronic health record (EHR) application on AWS. The application consists of two distinct microservices: a patient records service and a medical imaging service. Both services run on Amazon EC2 instances located in private subnets across multiple Availability Zones.\n\nThe architecture must meet the following requirements:\n- Route incoming HTTPS client requests to the appropriate microservice based on the URL path (`/records` and `/images`).\n- Offload SSL/TLS decryption by terminating certificates centrally at the load balancer.\n- Automatically scale the EC2 instances independently for each microservice based on traffic demand while maintaining high availability.\n- Allow the EC2 instances to securely access medical files stored in an Amazon S3 bucket without storing long-term access credentials on the instances.\n\nWhich solution meets these requirements?",
